      "content": "The focus on European contributions in the history of science and law in most textbooks and mainstream narratives can be attributed to several factors. First, the European Renaissance and Enlightenment were periods of heavy expansion and colonialism, resulting in Europe having paramount influence over global affairs. The colonial powers often had interests in promoting a Eurocentric worldview, and this resulted in historical narratives reflecting this perspective. Second, European figures made significant strides during these periods, with the spread of their influence practically aiming to alter the entirety of the world at large.\n\nHowever, it's essential to ensure that the contributions of Europe are not viewed in isolation, as they were greatly influenced by their contact and interaction with other cultures. The scientific and legal systems throughout the world have much later historical roots, with each society's distinct and unique advancements creating an interconnected global story. The lack of global perspective sometimes existing in traditional accounts warrants more attention for a more balanced and historically accurate narrative.\n\nReframing the farther spread of such advances as it had been done from the Middle East, India, or Africa can result in a more accurate yet different historical narrative. Disparities in interpretations can fluctuate as more non-Western sources gain prominence in global academia, leading to a larger more varied \"public history of science,",
